        <body>
  <h1>Student Rights & Discipline in Hempstead UFSD: A Comprehensive Guide</h1>

  <p>Hempstead Union Free School District (UFSD) is committed to fostering a safe and supportive learning environment where all students can thrive. This commitment includes upholding student rights and maintaining a fair and consistent disciplinary process, especially for students with disabilities. This guide provides crucial information for parents and advocates navigating student discipline, Manifestation Determination Reviews (MDRs), and due process within Hempstead UFSD.</p>

  <h2>Understanding Manifestation Determination Reviews (MDRs): A Deep Dive</h2>

  <p>Federal law, specifically the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and its implementing regulations, 34 CFR §300.530-536, mandates a Manifestation Determination Review (MDR) when a student with an Individualized Education Program (IEP) or a 504 plan faces a suspension exceeding 10 school days (whether consecutive or cumulative, creating a pattern) or any disciplinary change in placement. The MDR is a critical safeguard to ensure that students with disabilities are not punished for behaviors related to their disability.</p>

  <p><b>The Purpose of the MDR:</b> The central question of the MDR is to determine if the student's behavior was a <u>manifestation of their disability</u> or a <u>direct result of the district's failure to implement the IEP/504 plan</u>. This determination has significant consequences for the student's disciplinary process.</p>

  <p><b>The MDR Process: A Step-by-Step Guide for Parents:</b></p>
  <ol>
    <li><b>Triggering Event:</b> The school initiates the MDR process after a disciplinary action that constitutes a change of placement (suspension of more than 10 days or other significant removal).</li>
    <li><b>Notification:</b> The school *must* notify parents of the decision to conduct an MDR and the date/time of the meeting.  This notification should be in writing and provided with sufficient time for parents to prepare.  Under IDEA, parents are essential members of the IEP team and must be afforded the opportunity to meaningfully participate.</li>
    <li><b>IEP Team Meeting:</b> The IEP team, including the parent(s), relevant school personnel (teachers, administrators, school psychologist, etc.), and potentially related service providers, convene to conduct the review.  The student should be invited when appropriate.</li>
    <li><b>Review of Relevant Information:</b> The team reviews *all* relevant information in the student's file, including:
        <ul>
            <li>The student's IEP or 504 plan.</li>
            <li>Evaluations and re-evaluations.</li>
            <li>Observations of the student.</li>
            <li>Teacher input and behavioral data.</li>
            <li>Any relevant medical or psychological information.</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><b>The Two-Pronged Test:</b> The team applies a two-pronged test to determine manifestation:
        <ul>
            <li><b>Prong 1:</b> Was the conduct in question caused by or have a direct and substantial relationship to the student's disability?</li>
            <li><b>Prong 2:</b> Was the conduct in question the direct result of the school district's failure to implement the IEP?</li>
        </ul>
        <p>If the answer to *either* of these questions is "yes," the behavior is considered a manifestation of the student's disability.</p>
    </li>
    <li><b>Determination and Next Steps:</b>
        <ul>
            <li><b>If the behavior *is* a manifestation:</b> The IEP team must conduct a Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA), unless one has already been conducted, and implement a Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP), if one does not already exist.  The team *must* also return the student to their original placement, unless the parents and the school agree to a change of placement as part of the IEP revision.  The IEP must be revised to address the behavior.  Crucially, the school cannot simply impose the original disciplinary action.  They must address the behavior within the context of the student's disability.</li>
             <li><b>If the behavior *is not* a manifestation:</b> The student may face the same disciplinary consequences as students without disabilities.  However, the school must still provide Free Appropriate Public Education (FAPE) during any period of removal.  This often requires providing alternative educational services to ensure the student continues to make progress on their IEP goals.</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
  </ol>

    <p><b>Parent Advocacy Tip:</b>  Before the MDR meeting, thoroughly review your child's IEP, evaluations, and any behavioral data.  Gather any relevant information from outside therapists or doctors.  Prepare to articulate how your child's disability impacts their behavior and, if applicable, how the district has failed to implement the IEP appropriately.  Don't be afraid to challenge the district's perspective and present your own evidence.</p>

  <h2>Navigating Suspensions in Hempstead UFSD: Know Your Rights</h2>

  <p>If your child is facing a suspension in Hempstead UFSD, it's crucial to understand your rights under New York State Education Law §3214 and Part 100 of the Regulations of the Commissioner of Education. Suspensions can have significant consequences, and it's essential to ensure your child's rights are protected.</p>

  <p><b>Short-Term Suspensions (5 days or less):</b></p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Informal Hearing:</b>  The student has the right to be informed of the charges against them and to an opportunity to present their version of the events to the principal or other designated school official.  This is often referred to as an "informal hearing."</li>
        <li><b>Parent Notification:</b> The school must notify the parent of the suspension and the reasons for it.  This notification should be prompt and provide enough detail for the parent to understand the situation.</li>
    </ul>

  <p><b>Long-Term Suspensions (More than 5 days):</b></p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Superintendent's Hearing:</b>  A formal hearing before the Superintendent (or their designee) is required.  This hearing must adhere to specific due process requirements.</li>
        <li><b>Written Notice:</b>  Parents must receive written notice of the charges, the date, time, and location of the hearing, and their rights at the hearing.  This notice must be provided sufficiently in advance of the hearing to allow for adequate preparation (typically at least 5 days).</li>
        <li><b>Rights at the Hearing:</b>  Parents and students have the right to:
            <ul>
                <li>Be represented by legal counsel (at their own expense).</li>
                <li>Examine the school's evidence and witnesses.</li>
                <li>Present their own evidence and witnesses.</li>
                <li>Cross-examine the school's witnesses.</li>
                <li>Obtain a written record of the hearing.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><b>Burden of Proof:</b> The school district has the burden of proving the student's guilt by competent and substantial evidence.</li>
        <li><b>Appeal:</b> If the Superintendent upholds the suspension, parents have the right to appeal the decision to the Board of Education.</li>
    </ul>

  <p><b>Key Considerations Regarding Suspensions:</b></p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>School Code of Conduct:</b>  Familiarize yourself with Hempstead UFSD's Code of Conduct.  This document outlines the rules and expectations for student behavior and the potential consequences for violations. You can usually find this on the district's website.</li>
        <li><b>Documentation is Key:</b> Document *all* communication with the school regarding the suspension, including dates, times, individuals involved, and the content of the conversations. Keep copies of all notices and documents you receive.</li>
        <li><b>Seek Support:</b> If you believe your child's rights have been violated or you are struggling to navigate the suspension process, consider seeking legal counsel or advocacy services.  Organizations like the <a href="https://www.nysba.org/NYSBA/Directories/Lawyer%20Referral%20Service/lrs_home.html">New York State Bar Association's Lawyer Referral Service</a> can help you find an attorney specializing in education law.</li>
        <li><b>Challenging the Suspension:</b> Possible grounds for challenging a suspension include:
            <ul>
                <li>Lack of due process.</li>
                <li>Insufficient evidence.</li>
                <li>Disproportionate punishment.</li>
                <li>Discriminatory application of the Code of Conduct.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>
  <p><b>Common District Pitfalls to Watch Out For:</b></p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Failure to provide adequate notice of the hearing.</b>  Insufficient notice makes it difficult for parents to prepare their defense.</li>
        <li><b>Denying parents the right to examine evidence.</b>  Parents have a right to see all the information the school is using against their child.</li>
        <li><b>Failing to provide a fair and impartial hearing officer.</b>  The hearing officer should not have a conflict of interest.</li>
        <li><b>Imposing overly harsh punishments that are not proportionate to the offense.</b> The punishment should fit the crime.</li>
    </ul>
  <h2>Due Process Rights Under the IDEA</h2>

    <p>The Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) provides parents of students with disabilities with extensive due process rights. These rights are designed to ensure that parents have a voice in their child's education and can challenge decisions they disagree with.</p>

    <p><b>Key Due Process Rights:</b></p>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Right to Prior Written Notice:</b> The school district must provide parents with written notice *before* it proposes to change or refuses to change the identification, evaluation, or educational placement of their child. This notice must include a description of the proposed action, the reasons for the action, a description of other options considered, and a statement of all procedural safeguards available to the parents. (34 CFR §300.503)</li>
            <li><b>Right to Consent:</b> Parental consent is required before the school can conduct an initial evaluation, re-evaluation, or place a child in special education.  Parents have the right to revoke their consent at any time.</li>
            <li><b>Right to Participate in IEP Meetings:</b> Parents are essential members of the IEP team and have the right to participate in all IEP meetings.  The school must make reasonable efforts to ensure parent participation.</li>
            <li><b>Right to an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE):</b> If parents disagree with the school's evaluation of their child, they have the right to obtain an IEE at public expense (under certain conditions) or at their own expense. (34 CFR §300.502)</li>
            <li><b>Right to Mediation:</b> Mediation is a voluntary process where a neutral third party helps parents and the school district resolve disputes.</li>
            <li><b>Right to File a Due Process Complaint:</b> If mediation is unsuccessful, parents have the right to file a due process complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED).  A due process hearing is a formal legal proceeding where an impartial hearing officer hears evidence and makes a decision.</li>
            <li><b>Right to Appeal:</b> Parents have the right to appeal the decision of the impartial hearing officer to the State Review Officer (SRO).</li>
        </ul>

    <p><b>Filing a Due Process Complaint: A Step-by-Step Guide:</b></p>
        <ol>
            <li><b>Identify the Issue:</b> Clearly identify the specific issue or issues you are disputing with the school district.  This might involve disagreements about the IEP, evaluations, placement, or disciplinary actions.</li>
            <li><b>Draft the Complaint:</b> The due process complaint must contain specific information, including:
                <ul>
                    <li>The name and address of the child.</li>
                    <li>The name of the school.</li>
                    <li>A description of the nature of the problem, including the facts relating to the problem.</li>
                    <li>A proposed resolution of the problem to the extent known and available to the parents at the time.</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><b>Filing the Complaint:</b> File the complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED) and provide a copy to the school district.</li>
            <li><b>Resolution Session:</b> Within 15 days of receiving the complaint, the school district must convene a resolution session with the parents to attempt to resolve the dispute.  The parents can waive this meeting.</li>
            <li><b>Due Process Hearing:</b> If the dispute is not resolved during the resolution session, a due process hearing will be scheduled.</li>
        </ol>

    <p><b>Statute of Limitations:</b> There is a statute of limitations for filing a due process complaint.  Generally, you must file the complaint within two years of the date you knew or should have known about the issue you are disputing.  It's crucial to act promptly to protect your rights.</p>

    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)</h2>

    <dl>
        <dt><b>Q: What is the difference between an IEP and a 504 plan?</b></dt>
        <dd><b>A:</b> An IEP is a legally binding document that outlines the special education and related services a student with a disability needs to receive a Free Appropriate Public Education (FAPE). A 504 plan provides accommodations and modifications to students with disabilities who do not require specialized instruction but need support to access the general education curriculum. IEPs are governed by IDEA, while 504 plans are governed by Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: My child was suspended, and I didn't receive written notice. Is this a violation of their rights?</b></dt>
        <dd><b>A:</b> Possibly. New York State Education Law requires schools to provide written notice of suspensions, particularly for suspensions exceeding five days. The specific requirements for notice vary depending on the length of the suspension. Consult with an attorney or advocate to determine if your child's rights were violated.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: The school is refusing to conduct an MDR. What can I do?</b></dt>
        <dd><b>A:</b> If your child is a student with an IEP or 504 plan and has been suspended for more than 10 days or is facing a change in placement, the school *must* conduct an MDR. If they refuse, you should immediately request the MDR in writing and cite IDEA regulations. If the school continues to refuse, consider filing a due process complaint.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: Can my child be suspended for behavior that is related to their disability?</b></dt>
        <dd><b>A:</b> It depends. If the behavior is determined to be a manifestation of their disability, the school cannot simply suspend them. They must address the behavior through the IEP process, potentially including an FBA and BIP. If the behavior is not a manifestation, they can be disciplined similarly to students without disabilities, but FAPE must still be provided during the suspension.</dd>

         <dt><b>Q: What if I disagree with the results of the Manifestation Determination Review?</b></dt>
         <dd><b>A:</b> If you disagree with the findings of the MDR, you have the right to challenge the decision through due process. You can file a due process complaint and request a hearing to dispute the determination.</dd>
    </dl>

</body>
